;;; Strictness.mon --- A gentle tour of immediate evaluation

:author "Laluxx"
:version "1.0.0"
:keywords "strict eager immediate identity evaluation"

;;; Commentary:

 Strictness is the ordinary evaluation rhythm of Monad: compute an argument
 before passing it to a function. =Strict= makes that rhythm visible in a type.
 A value of type =Strict Int= does not contain a suspended integer computation;
 it contains an integer that has already been produced.

         expression ──evaluate──> result ──strict──> Strict value

 Consequently, =run-strict= performs no delayed work. It merely removes the
 wrapper. At the level of pure values, =Strict a= behaves like =a= with an
 explicit label:

                 run-strict (strict value)  ≃  value

 Why keep a wrapper that a compiler may erase? Because evaluation policy can
 then be stated locally in types. A tree may store recursive branches beneath
 =Strict= when every branch must be available immediately, while the same
 shape may use =Lazy= when branches should be produced on demand. Algorithms
 can talk about the structure without hiding its evaluation policy.

 Strictness is shallow. A =Strict (Lazy Int)= says that the outer Lazy value is
 available now; it does not force the computation inside that Lazy value.
 There is no implicit walk through lists, trees, tuples, or user data.

 The general =Evaluation= operation =defer= accepts a nullary callback. Under
 Strict, that callback is executed immediately and its result is wrapped.
 Under Lazy, the same callback is stored until demand. This callback boundary
 is the precise place where the two strategies differ.

 Since every inhabited Strict value already contains its result, demanding it
 is total. =total-strict= turns that operational fact into =TotalDemand=
 evidence. This is useful to generic code that must distinguish an ordinary
 possibly-diverging demand from one whose termination is known.

;;; Code:

import Control.Strict
import Control.Lazy
import Control.Evaluation

module StrictnessExample []


define strict-answer :: Strict Int
  strict (40 + 2)


define immediately-deferred :: Strict Int
  strict (40 + 2)


define strict-double :: Strict Int
  strict ((run-strict strict-answer) * 2)


define strict-around-lazy :: Strict (Lazy Int)
  strict (delay (40 + 2))


tests
  assert-eq (run-strict strict-answer) 42 "Strict contains an already evaluated value"
  assert-eq (run-strict immediately-deferred) 42 "Strict construction receives an evaluated value"
  assert-eq (run-strict strict-double) 84 "derived work is performed before Strict stores it"
  assert-eq (force (run-strict strict-around-lazy)) 42 "Strict is shallow and does not erase an inner Lazy"
